  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nToday and Tonight\n\nHigh pressure will provide dry weather through tonight. High\ntemperatures will range from the mid-50s to mid-60s. Low\ntemperatures will range from the lower 30s to lower 40s.\n\nFriday into Saturday Night\n\nThe surface high will move east from the Great Lakes into the\nMid-Atlantic region. As this high moves away, 850 mb moisture\ntransport will gradually increase ahead of a slowly moving \nshortwave trough embedded in the southwest flow aloft. This\nsystem will produce showers as it moves slowly northeast through\nthe region. Rainfall amounts will be up to a quarter of an inch.\nThe probabilities for greater than this total is up to 20\npercent north of Interstate 94 and 20 to 40 percent elsewhere.\n\nHigh temperatures will range from the mid-50s to lower 60s. Low\ntemperatures will range from the mid-30s to mid-40s on Friday\nnight and from the lower 40s to lower 50s on Saturday night.  \n\nSunday and Sunday night\n\nHigh pressure will provide dry weather. High temperatures will\nbe in the 60s. Low temperatures will range from the mid-40s to\nlower 50s.\n\nMonday into Wednesday\n\nThere continues to be much uncertainty on whether systems in \nthe northern and southern streams will phase somewhere from the\nNorthern Plains into the Great Lakes. If these systems phase,\nthere will be cyclogenesis across the region. At this time,\nthis would likely take place to our west and then the low would\neither track east across Upper Mississippi River Valley or north\nand northeast across northern Minnesota and the northern Great\nLakes. \n\nIf these systems do not phase, there will be 2 weaker low \npressure systems. One would move to our west and north and \nanother would track to our south an east. With these various \nscenarios, there is much uncertainty on temperatures and \nprecipitation amounts.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the 60s Monday and Tuesday and from\nthe mid-50s to mid-60s on Wednesday. Low temperatures will\nrange from the upper 40s to mid-50s on Monday night and from \nthe mid-40s to lower 50s on Tuesday night.",
